WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.540
Hey everyone i'm Abbey Sharpe welcome to Abbey's kitchen! I hope you are all

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:07.710
keeping yourself safe and healthy
wherever you are in the world as we're

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:12.870
definitely dealing with some very scary
times. Now considering the demographic of

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:16.289
people who follow me, today I want to
talk about how to manage your

00:00:16.289 --> 00:00:21.600
relationship with food during COVID 19,
as this is very likely being tested

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:26.279
right now and, you know, a lot of you have
asked me for this content in my comment

00:00:26.279 --> 00:00:29.609
section. Now as always my general
disclaimer that the information in this

00:00:29.609 --> 00:00:33.750
video is for education and entertainment
purposes only and is not in any way

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:38.100
intended to prescribe or diagnose, so
always reach out and speak to a health

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:43.079
care professional about your unique case.
Now if you are like me, your community is

00:00:43.079 --> 00:00:47.430
likely practicing social distancing
right now which means, of course, working

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:53.219
from home, probably at your kitchen table,
and close to food all day. Meanwhile,

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:58.109
you're no longer going to your HIIT or
spin class every day, or like ever, and

00:00:58.109 --> 00:01:03.750
your COVID 19 panic grocery shop may
have consisted of ice cream, chips, and

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:07.590
other convenience foods. As well as your
access to fresh fruits and vegetables

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:12.540
being limited, it's not surprising then
that we've seen an influx of memes and

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:19.259
internet jokes about the quarantine 15
or gaining the COVID 19 while we're all

00:01:19.259 --> 00:01:23.009
hunkering down. Now it might seem funny
to joke about gaining a lot of weight as

00:01:23.009 --> 00:01:27.540
your way to kind of lighten an already not
so bright mood, but for people who are

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:31.799
struggling with their relationship with
food, and their bodies in general, these

00:01:31.799 --> 00:01:36.299
are particularly challenging times.
Now no matter what your normal mental

00:01:36.299 --> 00:01:41.070
health status, nobody is really immune to
the psychological toll that social

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:45.420
isolation and Quarantine may have. A
review published in February in the

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:50.040
Lancet looked at 24 studies to evaluate
the psychological impact of quarantine

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:54.600
and found a number of problematic
outcomes including post-traumatic stress

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:59.340
disorder, confusion, and anger. The major variables involved in people's

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:03.619
experiences include fears around
infection and adequate supplies,

00:02:03.619 --> 00:02:09.179
financial loss, stigma, and boredom. All of
this is the perfect storm to drive even

00:02:09.179 --> 00:02:13.130
people with borderline or people with
relatively normal experiences

00:02:13.130 --> 00:02:17.810
with food into the extremes of
restriction and binging. But let's talk

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:22.580
specifically about this COVID 19 and our
relationship with food. So today I want

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:26.360
to chat about some of the major reasons
that we might see our social distancing

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:29.990
efforts perpetuate disordered or
emotional eating for those who are

00:02:29.990 --> 00:02:33.620
actively struggling with their
relationship with food, those who have

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:38.930
recovered to kind of fall into a relapse,
and for the vast majority of people who

00:02:38.930 --> 00:02:44.180
have felt even slightly impacted by diet
culture. As with this vaccine-less

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:50.030
coronavirus, nobody is immune. Reason
number one, the perception of scarcity.

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:53.600
Now we know from research on food
insecurity that the perception of food

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:58.280
scarcity can trigger disordered eating.
I've also talked a lot on my channel

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:02.900
about the perception of scarcity or
purposeful restriction and how that can

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:07.160
lead to binging or chaotic eating but
even if you're not on a weight-loss diet

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:12.980
or experiencing food insecurity, this
whole COVID 19 situation simulates

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:17.900
that experience to a tee. In the past, we
could go to the grocery store however

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:22.880
often we wanted to get a specific brand
or food item that we really want to eat,

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:28.130
we could plan a meal on the fly based on
what we felt like in that moment, and if

00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:32.090
we didn't feel like cooking we could
very easily order in or go out for a

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:36.680
meal literally within a moment's notice.
But we no longer have immediate access

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:40.760
to food that we want and it's getting
stressful. Instagram is littered with

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:44.570
images of empty grocery store shelves,
grocery delivery orders need to be

00:03:44.570 --> 00:03:48.560
placed like a week in advance and
there's no promise that half the things

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:52.970
on your list are gonna be available when
it's your time to shop. So whether you've

00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:57.380
struggled with an eating disorder in the
past or not, this perception of scarcity

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:02.420
or restriction may easily lead us to
compulsively overeat when we do have

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:07.520
food out of anticipatory fear that we
don't know when that chance is coming

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:10.670
again.
Now reason number two is temporary food

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:15.140
abundance so on the flip side there are
lots of people talking about their epic

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:19.489
food hauls and coronavirus snacks, and
while a lot of people who struggle with

00:04:19.489 --> 00:04:24.050
eating may limit the amount of food in
their home at one time, the grocery store

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:29.250
hauls that this situation has incited
may have your pantry and fridge stuffed to

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:33.840
the brim. This easy access to food
supplies can very easily trigger binge

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.010
eating episodes when there's the
constant reminder of the impending food

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:41.370
shortage or lack of access in the back
of our brain.

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:46.650
Number three lack, of access to specialty
diet foods. Now for those who are

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:50.850
strictly following a really strict keto
diet or Paleo diet or whatever and

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:56.010
relying on specialty foods and
ingredients to maintain that diet, it may

00:04:56.010 --> 00:05:00.300
be especially stressful to imagine that
this may not be so easy

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:05.430
while in isolation with limited grocery
access. You might not be able to get your

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:10.380
specialty low carb high protein bars and
maybe you can't choose the leanest cut

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:15.240
of meat at the store. This can be highly
stressful for people. Reason number four

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:21.000
is no more gym. A lot of people use
exercise, not just for their mental

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:25.680
sanity, but also for those who have a
history disordered eating it may be use

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:29.820
as a way to balance dietary intake. With
all of your fitness classes and gyms

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:34.410
closed and most of us feeling really
cooped up in tiny apartments, the

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:39.840
inability to exercise and move may spark
massive fear of weight gain which, in

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:45.780
turn, may perpetuate caloric restriction.
Number five, the excessive food talk. As I

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:49.860
mentioned, there is a lot of talk about
food right now - lack of food in the

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:55.919
grocery stores, quarantine snacks, gaining
the COVID 19, what to make with canned

00:05:55.919 --> 00:06:01.229
lentils etc if you're online and, let's
be real what else is there to do right

00:06:01.229 --> 00:06:06.630
now? You cannot escape the world's
obsession about what to eat and if you

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:10.260
have a history of emotional or
disordered eating, this can definitely

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:14.820
set you off. Number six,
being in close quarters with triggering

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:19.289
family. Now being in close proximity to
family members with no escape or

00:06:19.289 --> 00:06:24.060
external outlets can be highly stressful
and triggering for a lot of people. Often

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:28.140
times, it's these close individuals who
may actually have stimulated the

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:32.010
problematic eating in the first place
even if you were to feel like you had

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:35.940
gotten your, you know, mental health in a
really good place before coronavirus

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:39.970
it's very possible that this could
reignite a lot of problems.

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:44.620
Number seven, stress and emotional eating. As I've discussed on my channel many

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:49.840
times, a lot of us turn to food to deal
with negative emotions. That's totally

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:54.100
normal and it's totally normal and
common to be turning to food in these

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:59.950
uncertain times. For a lot of you, it
really may be the only friend we have in

00:06:59.950 --> 00:07:02.980
the home but when it becomes our only
coping mechanism

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:07.060
we may see symptoms that range from
severe caloric restriction to total

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:11.590
binge eating and we'll talk more about
that very soon. Number eight, mandated

00:07:11.590 --> 00:07:16.810
isolation. Social isolation in general is
a really common feature of eating

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:21.310
disorders as it makes it easier to avoid
social eating experiences and body

00:07:21.310 --> 00:07:26.050
exposure. Which can make a person more
likely to maintain or fall back into

00:07:26.050 --> 00:07:30.610
disorderly behaviors. Now when people are
in recovery or in therapy they are

00:07:30.610 --> 00:07:35.890
literally told not to socially isolate,
you know, to be as social as possible

00:07:35.890 --> 00:07:42.220
when they can. But here we are unable to
do anything else but be on our own.

00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:47.020
And finally, the lack of access to
therapy. Those who are used to getting

00:07:47.020 --> 00:07:52.030
regular access to therapy for anxiety
depression and disorderly thoughts may

00:07:52.030 --> 00:07:56.919
be no longer able to do that thanks to
social distancing protocol. Hence why

00:07:56.919 --> 00:08:00.850
this is becoming a big problem. Now to
help everyone during these uncertain

00:08:00.850 --> 00:08:04.930
times, I had a chat with two different
intuitive eating dietitian colleagues

00:08:04.930 --> 00:08:08.590
about their experiences and what they're
telling their patients in tough times

00:08:08.590 --> 00:08:12.970
like these. I was joined separately by
Ellen Silverman and Jesse Haggerty, two

00:08:12.970 --> 00:08:17.500
dietitians who work with eating disorder
patients. So thank you both so much for

00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:21.310
joining me. Let's take a look at those
interviews. Tell me a bit about some of

00:08:21.310 --> 00:08:25.300
the themes that you're seeing in your
now virtual appointments. Over the last

00:08:25.300 --> 00:08:29.020
week it's really changed. At the
beginning of last week most people were

00:08:29.020 --> 00:08:35.050
like, you know, concerned about their work,
concerned about, you know, how are they

00:08:35.050 --> 00:08:40.990
going to keep going. This week it's kind
of changed to reality, like, again a lot

00:08:40.990 --> 00:08:45.130
of my clients are very food insecure so
they're just like, you know, they're

00:08:45.130 --> 00:08:48.880
sitting at home and here they are
dealing with their eating disorder and

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:53.670
they don't know. They're looking in their
cabinets, they're looking in the refrigerator.

00:08:53.670 --> 00:08:58.560
They don't know what to eat and so it's
changed to that. Another thing that

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:01.830
I'm noticing is that some clients are
like, you know, concerned about their not

00:09:01.830 --> 00:09:06.120
being able to pay for their visits. You
know, is it more important to go

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:10.709
for their therapists or, you know, go to
their dietician for a virtual visit.

00:09:10.709 --> 00:09:15.510
That's another running theme and then a
lot of them are having a lot of issues

00:09:15.510 --> 00:09:21.029
around isolation. So when they're in
isolation their diseases are more

00:09:21.029 --> 00:09:25.170
rampant and their voices are hard to
ignore

00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:30.350
so that you know their eating disorder
voice comes out loud and proud and

00:09:30.350 --> 00:09:35.760
they're not able to fight it. And it's
interesting because some of them have

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:42.120
been in really good stable recovery for
a long time and thoughts of like, you

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:46.350
know, their childhood and not having food
and you know that stuff is coming up

00:09:46.350 --> 00:09:50.250
from a deep place that they've already
worked on maybe, but now it's coming up

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:56.279
like in a different capacity. A lot of my
clients are student that have now gone from

00:09:56.279 --> 00:10:01.740
living on campus to living with their
families and they're not in charge of

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:04.950
when they're eating or what they're
eating right now so that can always be

00:10:04.950 --> 00:10:10.020
really stressful. A lot of my clients
have started working at home all of a

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:16.110
sudden. I'm used to working at home my
work time but for people who are not used to

00:10:16.110 --> 00:10:20.279
working at home it can be stressful to
feel like, on one hand you have this like

00:10:20.279 --> 00:10:24.779
constant access to food in your house,
and then on the other hand you're kind

00:10:24.779 --> 00:10:29.550
of limited to how often you leave your
house to maybe procure more food. I know

00:10:29.550 --> 00:10:33.270
that in a lot of areas there are a lot
of grocery stores that have very limited

00:10:33.270 --> 00:10:38.520
amounts of food available. So yeah it's
pretty stressful no matter what

00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:43.649
situation you in right now. So how can we eat intuitively when the food that we have

00:10:43.649 --> 00:10:49.230
in our freezer or a pantry is maybe not
what we really want. I'd go back to the

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:55.170
hierarchy of needs and nourishment being
one of them and when they're, you know,

00:10:55.170 --> 00:10:59.220
I'm kind of encouraging them
to plan a little bit. Like I've had to do

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:03.329
I've had to see what's in my cabinets in
my refrigerator and kind of look

00:11:03.329 --> 00:11:06.769
"okay what do I have I have some ground
beef

00:11:06.769 --> 00:11:11.239
I have some turkey breast" what do I have to put together a little meal plan. So for

00:11:11.239 --> 00:11:16.610
dinner we're gonna have this sort of if
that lasts and just go a few days out

00:11:16.610 --> 00:11:22.249
not to think about it too much. Um, I also
have them kind of reevaluate their label

00:11:22.249 --> 00:11:27.649
on the foods they don't want to eat like
why do you have that label on it? What's

00:11:27.649 --> 00:11:31.850
that all about? And can we think about it
a little bit differently? Can you make

00:11:31.850 --> 00:11:35.929
something out of those foods that you
have that you don't want to eat? Can you

00:11:35.929 --> 00:11:40.309
make it into something you would eat? Like
you might want so instead of having, I

00:11:40.309 --> 00:11:43.459
don't know,
tuna fish on crackers can you put some

00:11:43.459 --> 00:11:48.230
peanut butter and jelly on some crackers
and have a glass of milk? Can you

00:11:48.230 --> 00:11:52.220
scramble up some eggs? If they say "I don't feel like eggs" well how about if you stick

00:11:52.220 --> 00:11:55.759
some cheese in it and maybe some of that
summer sausage that's in the back of

00:11:55.759 --> 00:11:57.649
your refrigerator that can last a long
time.

00:11:57.649 --> 00:12:02.299
Maybe sticking that in it so rethinking
you know the basic reason why they're

00:12:02.299 --> 00:12:06.679
not wanting the food and kind of seeing
if we can kind of change it around a

00:12:06.679 --> 00:12:12.439
little bit. I think that intuitive eating
generally gets this reputation of being

00:12:12.439 --> 00:12:17.569
this like eat whatever you want whenever
you want. For the work that I do with my

00:12:17.569 --> 00:12:22.220
clients, I think that it is always very
rooted and first and foremost like

00:12:22.220 --> 00:12:26.749
gentleness and self compassion. So just
being a little bit kinder to yourself

00:12:26.749 --> 00:12:31.999
now, knowing that there's never really
such thing as perfect eating but right

00:12:31.999 --> 00:12:40.730
now like give yourself permission to do
like a c+ job around food. But I think that the

00:12:40.730 --> 00:12:46.160
things that you can focus on are
honoring your hunger, making time to eat

00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:49.730
throughout the day. I mean I typically
recommend clients trying to make space

00:12:49.730 --> 00:12:55.879
to eat every three to four hours, trying
to get some protein, some carbs, some fat.

00:12:55.879 --> 00:13:01.699
There's a lot of flexibility that you
can do within those parameters and

00:13:01.699 --> 00:13:06.769
especially if you're working from home,
like taking breaks to eat and fuel

00:13:06.769 --> 00:13:10.879
yourself I think that when you don't
have those natural breaks built into

00:13:10.879 --> 00:13:17.679
your day with commuting or lunch with
coworkers, it can be really difficult to

00:13:17.679 --> 00:13:21.079
remember to eat sometimes.

00:13:21.079 --> 00:13:25.430
What are some of your top tips for avoiding falling into either restrict or binge like tendencies during these times?

00:13:25.430 --> 00:13:31.190
I am a big person
about self-compassion and self grace and

00:13:31.190 --> 00:13:36.620
so again trying not to beat
themselves over the head during a very

00:13:36.620 --> 00:13:41.899
stressful time and letting them know
that we all binge. For example, like we

00:13:41.899 --> 00:13:46.430
all have that tendency to want to do
something that will comfort. So no

00:13:46.430 --> 00:13:52.250
judgment on that. Restriction, again, I'm
honoring where they're at I'm

00:13:52.250 --> 00:13:55.880
having them acknowledge why they're
there why do they want to restrict?

00:13:55.880 --> 00:13:59.660
Is it because they're scared they're
gonna run out of food? Let's acknowledge

00:13:59.660 --> 00:14:04.240
that you know. But then I have them
kind of turn it around and like and

00:14:04.240 --> 00:14:08.449
again some of these clients have had
some tools in their toolbox that they're

00:14:08.449 --> 00:14:14.089
not using so I'm like okay let's get
that out let's redirect but I want you

00:14:14.089 --> 00:14:18.709
to feel that feeling. I don't want you to
push it down so let's talk about it.

00:14:18.709 --> 00:14:22.370
I have them journals some of these
feelings that are coming out around

00:14:22.370 --> 00:14:28.579
their binging and restricting. If they're
still on a meal plan from IOP or you

00:14:28.579 --> 00:14:32.449
know in my practice I don't really have
meal plans but I might have to do that I

00:14:32.449 --> 00:14:37.850
have to go back to their meal plan for
some security. It's a lot of this has to

00:14:37.850 --> 00:14:41.839
do with loss of control of which they
already have issues with loss of control

00:14:41.839 --> 00:14:45.920
and they have no security. When are they
getting groceries? How are they gonna get

00:14:45.920 --> 00:14:49.850
out to get groceries? Who's gonna get
groceries for me? What am I gonna get

00:14:49.850 --> 00:14:53.839
when I get there? Or what are they gonna bring home when I

00:14:53.839 --> 00:14:58.100
give a list? So again addressing that
these are real concerns not to fooling

00:14:58.100 --> 00:15:02.420
them and saying oh it'll be alright. For
a lot of us it's not alright.

00:15:02.420 --> 00:15:06.680
So its acknowledging that we're feeling that too but let's

00:15:06.680 --> 00:15:09.350
kind of walk through some of the tools
that you've used in the past to get

00:15:09.350 --> 00:15:15.170
through it and and then again having
them take a pause and do some grounding

00:15:15.170 --> 00:15:21.019
around meals like if you're really
anxious let's kind of come back to the

00:15:21.019 --> 00:15:26.089
meal in a little while and look at it a
little bit differently. Let's do some

00:15:26.089 --> 00:15:30.050
grounding and breathing exercises or
whatever they can do. I mean, again, I

00:15:30.050 --> 00:15:33.800
think like first and foremost have
a little bit of gentleness with yourself

00:15:33.800 --> 00:15:39.670
around this. We're all trying to figure
this out, this is not easy for everyone

00:15:39.670 --> 00:15:45.949
but, again, I think really trying to build
in those breaks throughout the day so

00:15:45.949 --> 00:15:50.870
every few hours if you're making a
schedule for yourself,  like building in a

00:15:50.870 --> 00:15:57.019
time to like have a snack, have something
to eat. If you have access to maybe like

00:15:57.019 --> 00:16:02.089
more fun foods that you may normally
consider off-limits, like making time to

00:16:02.089 --> 00:16:06.769
have those foods so they don't feel like
they're in your house but you're also

00:16:06.769 --> 00:16:10.100
not allowed to have them. Like allow
yourself to have the foods that you

00:16:10.100 --> 00:16:17.089
enjoy. Umm yeah and I think that, like I said
before, just trying to have a mix and having

00:16:17.089 --> 00:16:22.760
a variety of foods as much as possible.
And I think now even though our options

00:16:22.760 --> 00:16:28.160
are more limited it is probably a way to
start adding more variety into your diet

00:16:28.160 --> 00:16:31.190
if maybe you weren't someone who
normally went to the frozen food section

00:16:31.190 --> 00:16:36.319
or to the canned food section. I know
that there's like so many ways you can

00:16:36.319 --> 00:16:40.639
get creative with foods like that we
don't normally think about. Totally agree

00:16:40.639 --> 00:16:44.930
and a lot of us actually get into food
routines and for some people those

00:16:44.930 --> 00:16:50.420
routines can be disorderly so, for some,
this may actually be used to help us

00:16:50.420 --> 00:16:55.430
kind of edge into new food experiences.
Now, what other coping strategies can

00:16:55.430 --> 00:16:59.930
